RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"guess", nav:"The guessing game",
title:"The guessing game — <em>your first real program</em>",
lead:"Type, if, loop, input — they all work together now. Three steps, one game.",
words:[
"<b>Step 1 — ask.</b> <code class='inline'>println!(\"guess 1-10:\")</code> shows the question. Then <code class='inline'>io::stdin().read_line(&mut g)</code> reads the answer. It puts the typed text into the box named <code class='inline'>g</code>.",
"<b>Step 2 — clean and change.</b> The text comes with the Enter key still on it: <code class='inline'>\"5\\n\"</code>. <code class='inline'>trim()</code> cuts the Enter off. Now <code class='inline'>\"5\"</code> is left. <code class='inline'>parse()</code> turns it into a real number.",
"<b>Step 3 — answer.</b> Three doors: <code class='inline'>if g == secret</code> prints win and <code class='inline'>break</code>s out. <code class='inline'>else if g < secret</code> prints small. <code class='inline'>else</code> prints big. All of it sits inside <code class='inline'>loop</code>. It repeats until you win.",
"So: <b>read</b> a number, <b>check</b> it, <b>repeat</b>. That is most of every program you will ever read. Flip the cards, then read the real code."
],
analogy:[["A quiz: \"I'm thinking of a number…\"","=","A loop that repeats the round"],["\"Too big — try again\"","=","An else-if that sends you around again"],["The secret number (7, fixed for now)","=","secret = 7 (dice come later)"]],
coach:"Step 1 uses <code>&mut</code>. Do not worry if it feels new. Copy it for now. The Core ideas chapter teaches <code>&mut</code> slowly. For now: copy, run, break it, fix it.",
widget:{type:"gym", q:[
{q:"Which word makes a box changeable, so the answer can go in?", hint:"the box starts with let…",
a:"mut — let mut g, not let g.", why:"No mut, no change. The keyboard must write into the box."},
{q:"Why call trim() before parse()?", hint:"read_line keeps one extra thing",
a:"read_line keeps the Enter key (\\n). trim() cuts it: \"5\\n\" turns into \"5\".", why:"No trim, no number. parse sees \"5\\n\" and stops."},
{q:"Where does the round start again after small or big?", hint:"the walls that repeat",
a:"loop holds the whole game. break after win! is the only way out.", why:"loop repeats until break. small and big just go back to the top."}
]},
code:{
copy:`use std::io;
fn main() {
    let secret = 7; // note: the real book rolls dice. We use 7 first. Easy.
    loop {
        println!("guess 1-10:");
        let mut g = String::new();
        io::stdin().read_line(&mut g).expect("no read");
        let g: u32 = g.trim().parse().expect("type a number");
        if g == secret { println!("win!"); break; }
        else if g < secret { println!("small"); }
        else { println!("big"); }
    }
}`,
lines:[
["use std::io;","the keyboard room"],
["let secret = 7; // note: the real book rolls dice. We use 7 first. Easy.","the secret number — fixed for now"],
["loop {","this wall repeats forever… until break"],
["    println!(\"guess 1-10:\");","invite the player in"],
["    let mut g = String::new();","a new, empty, changeable text box"],
["    io::stdin().read_line(&mut g).expect(\"no read\");","the keyboard writes into the box"],
["    let g: u32 = g.trim().parse().expect(\"type a number\");","same name, new box: text becomes a number"],
["    if g == secret { println!(\"win!\"); break; }","the win door — and the escape"],
["    else if g < secret { println!(\"small\"); }","too low, round restarts"],
["    else { println!(\"big\"); }","too high, round restarts"],
["    }",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Same name twice — on purpose:",b:"Rust lets you reuse a name: first <code class='inline'>let g = String::new();</code>, later <code class='inline'>let g = g.trim().parse()…</code>. The second box <b>hides</b> the first. Same name, new value. This is called shadowing."},
why:{kick:"where you meet this in real code",
h:"Most software is this loop. Wait for input. Check it. Answer. Repeat.",
points:[
"<b>Bad input is normal.</b> Real tools do not crash on bad input. They print 'type a number' and ask again. Later lessons teach the no-crash way.",
"<b>if-else-if fits three doors.</b> win, small, big — one chain, read top to bottom. You meet match in the Core ideas chapter.",
"<b>Shadowing keeps names short.</b> g, then g again. No messy names like g, g2, g_final. Same name, new box."
]},
done:"Built a guessing game"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"ownership", nav:"Ownership",
title:"Ownership — <em>the one who owns it</em>",
lead:"This is the famous Rust idea. It sounds hard. It is a simple story about who owns what.",
words:[
"<b>Rule 1:</b> Every value has exactly <b>one owner</b> — one box that holds it. <b>Rule 2:</b> only one owner at a time. <b>Rule 3:</b> when the owner goes away, Rust throws the value away for you.",
"<b>The move:</b> <code class='inline'>let s2 = s1;</code> does NOT copy. It <b>moves</b> the value from box s1 to box s2. Box s1 is now empty. Use s1 after that? Rust says NO. This stops bugs where two boxes change the same text.",
"<b>Small values are different:</b> numbers like <code class='inline'>i32</code> get <b>copied</b>. Both boxes keep a copy. No problem.",
"Try the toy: move the text, then try the old box. See what happens."
],
widget:{type:"ownplay"},
code:{
copy:`let s1 = String::from("hello");
let s2 = s1;           // s1 MOVES to s2
// println!("{}", s1); // ERROR! s1 is empty now
let a = 5;             // numbers are different...
let b = a;             // ...they get COPIED, a stays fine`,
lines:[
["let s1 = String::from(\"hello\");","s1 owns the text"],
["let s2 = s1;","the text MOVES to s2. s1 is now empty."],
["// println!(\"{}\", s1);","ERROR — using an empty box. Bodyguard!",null],
["let a = 5;","a number..."],
["let b = a;","...gets COPIED. a keeps its 5. No error."]
]},
tip:{h:"The question that saves you:",b:"When you see <code class='inline'>let x = y;</code>, ask: \"String or number?\" Strings move. Numbers copy. That is most of ownership, right there."},
done:"Understands ownership"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"borrow", nav:"Borrowing",
title:"Borrowing — <em>look, don't take</em>",
lead:"If everything moved, using data twice would be impossible. That's where borrowing comes in.",
words:[
"A <b>reference</b> <code class='inline'>&s</code> means: \"may I look at your book? I will not take it.\" You borrow the data. The owner keeps it. Nothing moves.",
"One rule for borrowing: <b>many readers, OR one writer — never both.</b> Many people may read (<code class='inline'>&</code>) the same book. Or one person may write (<code class='inline'>&mut</code>). A writer plus a reader at the same time = Rust says no.",
"Why so strict? In other languages, two people changing one thing brings crashes. Rust makes that impossible — before the program runs.",
"Try the tests below: mix it wrong and see what happens.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>many readers is fine. One writer is fine. Never both at once.</i>"
],
widget:{type:"borrowplay"},
code:{
copy:`let mut book = String::from("Rust");
let reader1 = &book;   // look at it (borrow) — ok
let reader2 = &book;   // another reader — ok
// let writer = &mut book; // ERROR while readers exist!
let writer = &mut book;   // no readers now — ok
writer.push_str("!");     // the writer can change it`,
lines:[
["let mut book = String::from(\"Rust\");","the owner of the data"],
["let reader1 = &book;","borrow #1 — looking only"],
["let reader2 = &book;","borrow #2 — many readers are fine"],
["// let writer = &mut book;","ERROR: can't write while readers read!"],
["let writer = &mut book;","readers gone — now one writer is allowed"],
["writer.push_str(\"!\");","the writer changes the text"]
]},
tip:{h:"One picture:",b:"& is reading glasses. &mut is a pen. You may hand out many glasses, or one pen — never both at once. That is the whole borrow checker."},
done:"Understands borrowing"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"shapes", nav:"Arrays, Tuples, Slices",
title:"Arrays, tuples & slices — <em>the shelf, the box set, the window</em>",
lead:"You already have Vec — a shelf that can grow. Now meet three more shapes for storing things.",
words:[
"<b>Array</b> <code class='inline'>[i32; 3]</code>: a shelf with a <b>fixed size</b> — you pick the size first, and it never changes. Like lockers fixed to a wall. Rust must know the size before it runs.",
"<b>Tuple</b> <code class='inline'>(i32, &str, bool)</code>: one box where each slot can hold a <i>different kind</i> of thing. <code class='inline'>(\"hello\", 5, true)</code> is fine. Good for small groups that belong together.",
"<b>Slice</b> <code class='inline'>&[T]</code>: no new box at all — it is a <b>window</b> into someone else's shelf. No owning, no copying. That is why it starts with <code class='inline'>&</code>: a view, not a shelf.",
"Few fixed items → array. Mixed group → tuple. Look at part of a list, no copy → slice."
],
analogy:[["A wall of lockers (decided up front, never changes)","=","Array [i32; 3]"],["A mixed snack box (sweet + salty together)","=","Tuple (i32, &str, bool)"],["Looking through a shop window (you see it, you don't own it)","=","Slice &[T]"]],
widget:{type:"shaperack"},
code:{
copy:`let numbers: [i32; 3] = [5, 9, 1];  // locker wall, 3 slots
let trio = (5, "rust", true);          // snack box, mixed kinds
let window = &numbers[0..2];           // a window over slots 0..2`,
lines:[
["let numbers: [i32; 3] = [5, 9, 1];","locker wall — exactly 3 slots, all numbers"],
["let trio = (5, \"rust\", true);","snack box — a number, text and truth together"],
["let window = &numbers[0..2];","a window showing slots 0 and 1 — no copying!"]
]},
tip:{h:"Spot the &:",b:"When you see <code class='inline'>&</code> in a type — like <code class='inline'>&[T]</code> or <code class='inline'>&str</code> — it means \"a view, not mine\". You look, you do not take. This idea matters a lot in the ownership lessons."},
done:"Knows arrays, tuples, slices"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"strings", nav:"Text",
title:"Text — <em>String vs &str</em>",
lead:"Computers love text. Rust has two kinds, and it's simpler than it sounds.",
words:[
"<b>String</b> is text that can <i>grow</i> — you can add letters. It can change. <b>&str</b> (say \"string slice\") is text that just <i>is there</i> — like words on a sign. You read it. You do not change it.",
"To grow a String, use <code class='inline'>push_str</code>: <code class='inline'>my_name.push_str(\"suffix\");</code> — and it must be <code class='inline'>mut</code>. Growing is changing!",
"To put values inside printed text, use <b>placeholders</b>: <code class='inline'>println!(\"Hi {}, you are {}\", name, age);</code> — each <code class='inline'>{}</code> means \"put the next value here\"."
],
widget:{type:"strings"},
code:{
copy:`let mut greeting = String::from("Hello");
greeting.push_str(", world!");          // grows the text
println!("{}", greeting);              // prints: Hello, world!
let label: &str = "welcome sign";      // fixed text, just read it`,
lines:[
["let mut greeting = String::from(\"Hello\");","make a growable String — note mut!"],
["greeting.push_str(\", world!\");","glue more text onto it — it grows"],
["println!(\"{}\", greeting);","{} is a placeholder for greeting"],
["let label: &str = \"welcome sign\";","&str = fixed sign text, just read it"]
]},
tip:{h:"The simplest rule:",b:"Need to <b>change</b> or <b>build</b> text → String. Just a fixed text to read → &str. Not sure? Rust errors tell you a lot — read them."},
done:"Understands String vs &str"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"structs", nav:"Structs",
title:"Structs — <em>the ID card</em>",
lead:"Sometimes one box isn't enough — you need a card with many labelled slots. That's a struct.",
words:[
"A <b>struct</b> is a card you design yourself: you pick the named slots it has. <code class='inline'>struct Player { name: String, level: i32 }</code> — every player has a name and a level.",
"To make one: <code class='inline'>let p = Player { name: ..., level: 1 };</code>. To read a slot, use a dot: <code class='inline'>p.name</code>. To change it, the card must be <code class='inline'>mut</code>: <code class='inline'>p.level += 1;</code>.",
"Why? Programs track things with many facts — players, files, orders. A struct keeps all facts of one thing on one card.",
"Build your own ID card below.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a struct holds many named facts in one box.</i>"
],
widget:{type:"structbuild"},
code:{
copy:`struct Player {
    name: String,
    level: i32,
}
let mut p = Player {
    name: String::from("Quiet"),
    level: 1,
};
p.level += 1;   // level up! card must be mut`,
lines:[
["struct Player {","define the card template"],
["    name: String,","slot 1: a name"],
["    level: i32,","slot 2: a level number"],
["}","template done"],
["let mut p = Player {","make a real card — lid open"],
["    name: String::from(\"Quiet\"),","fill slot 1"],
["    level: 1,","fill slot 2"],
["};","card complete"],
["p.level += 1;","dot = reach into the card. Level up!"]
]},
tip:{h:"Struct or enum?",b:"One thing with many facts (a player) → struct. A choice between options → enum, next lesson."},
done:"Builds structs"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"methods", nav:"Methods — impl",
title:"Methods — <em>the ID card gets superpowers</em>",
lead:"struct is the card. impl is where you give the card superpowers.",
words:[
"A struct holds data — it is the ID card. But a card that does nothing is boring. <b>Methods</b> are functions that live <i>on</i> the struct: <code class='inline'>player.hit(10)</code> — the player takes damage.",
"The <code class='inline'>impl</code> block is where methods live: <code class='inline'>impl Player { ... }</code>. Each method inside gets the card's own data as <code class='inline'>self</code>.",
"Two kinds: methods that <b>change</b> the card take <code class='inline'>&mut self</code> (and the card must be <code class='inline'>let mut</code>). Methods that only <b>read</b> take <code class='inline'>&self</code>. Same borrow rules as always.",
"This is how Rust gives things behavior — no classes, just structs + impl. You will see this same shape on every type, forever.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a method lives on the struct — self is the card it works on.</i>"
],
analogy:[["The ID card (name, photo, data)","=","struct Player"],["The clubhouse with ability manuals","=","impl Player { ... }"],["Reading the card / updating the card","=","&self (look) / &mut self (change)"]],
widget:{type:"upgrade"},
code:{
copy:`impl Player {
    fn name(&self) -> String {        // looks at the card
        self.name.clone()
    }
    fn hit(&mut self, dmg: i32) {     // changes the card
        self.hp -= dmg;
    }
}
let mut rusty = Player { name: "Rusty".to_string(), hp: 100 };
rusty.hit(10);       // hp is now 90`,
lines:[
["impl Player {","the clubhouse — Player's superpowers live here"],
["    fn name(&self) -> String {","a reading method: &self = look, don't take"],
["        self.name.clone()","hand back a copy of the name"],
["    fn hit(&mut self, dmg: i32) {","a changing method: &mut self = update the card"],
["        self.hp -= dmg;","deal the damage"],
["let mut rusty = Player { name: \"Rusty\".to_string(), hp: 100 };","a fresh card — must be mut for hit()"],
["rusty.hit(10);","call the superpower: hp 100 → 90"]
]},
tip:{h:"self = the card itself:",b:"<code class='inline'>&self</code> means \"read my own card\". <code class='inline'>&mut self</code> means \"change my own card\". Borrow rules work the same inside methods."},
done:"Writes methods"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"enums", nav:"Enums & Option",
title:"Enums & Option — <em>choices, and maybe-nothing</em>",
lead:"Enums are about choices. Option is about 'maybe there's something here, maybe not'.",
words:[
"An <b>enum</b> lists the allowed choices: <code class='inline'>enum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }</code> — the light can only be one of these. Never purple. The compiler checks it.",
"<b>Option</b> is Rust's built-in \"maybe\" box. It is <code class='inline'>Some(value)</code> — something is inside — or <code class='inline'>None</code> — empty. Like a pocket: a coin, or nothing.",
"Why must you think about None? In other languages, \"nothing\" crashes the program at the worst time. Rust makes you handle it first.",
"Pull the lever: sometimes a coin, sometimes nothing — match handles both, no crash.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>an enum is one box with named choices.</i>"
],
widget:{type:"optionplay"},
code:{
copy:`enum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }

let pocket: Option<i32> = Some(7);
match pocket {
    Some(c) => println!("coin: {}", c),
    None    => println!("nothing there"),
}`,
lines:[
["enum Light { Red, Yellow, Green }","the allowed choices for a light"],
["","(blank line — just for eyes)"],
["let pocket: Option<i32> = Some(7);","a maybe-box, holding 7"],
["match pocket {","sort the maybe-box"],
["    Some(c) => println!(\"coin: {}\", c),","something inside → show the coin"],
["    None    => println!(\"nothing there\"),","empty → say so"],
["}","sorted"]
]},
tip:{h:"Rule of thumb:",b:"Might it not exist — a file, a list entry? Rust gives you an Option. <code class='inline'>Some</code> = it is there. <code class='inline'>None</code> = it is not. Handle both, and missing things never crash you."},
done:"Understands enums & Option"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"match", nav:"match",
title:"match — <em>the sorting machine</em>",
lead:"if is a single door. match is a whole sorting machine with labelled baskets.",
words:[
"<code class='inline'>match</code> looks at one value and drops it in the basket that fits. Each <b>arm</b> is one basket: a label and an answer.",
"The <code class='inline'>_</code> arm is the <b>catch-all basket</b> — \"anything else goes here\". Rust wants a place for every value. Nothing ever falls on the floor.",
"match instead of if? If you add a new choice later, match <i>forces</i> you to handle it. No surprises.",
"Click a color — see the machine sort it."
],
widget:{type:"matchplay"},
code:{
copy:`let color = "green";
let msg = match color {
    "red"   => "stop",
    "green" => "go",
    _       => "wait for it...",
};
println!("{}", msg);`,
lines:[
["let color = \"green\";","the value we're sorting"],
["let msg = match color {","start the sorting machine"],
["    \"red\"   => \"stop\",","basket labeled 'red' → gives \"stop\""],
["    \"green\" => \"go\",","basket labeled 'green' → gives \"go\""],
["    _       => \"wait for it...\",","catch-all basket → anything else"],
["};","the machine hands back the result"],
["println!(\"{}\", msg);","print what came out"]
]},
tip:{h:"The arrow pattern:",b:"Every arm looks like <code class='inline'>label => answer</code>. Read it as: \"if it is this label, give back this answer\"."},
done:"Understands match"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"modules", nav:"Modules, crates, packages",
title:"Modules & packages — <em>the house has rooms</em>",
lead:"One file grows into a house: rooms (modules), a front door (main.rs), and borrowed boxes on shelves (crates).",
words:[
"A <b>crate</b> is one book. A <b>package</b> is the box the book ships in — one Cargo.toml card, at most one library, many binaries. A <b>module</b> is a chapter.",
"A <b>binary</b> crate is a book you read yourself. It has <code class='inline'>fn main</code>. <code class='inline'>src/main.rs</code> is page one. A <b>library</b> crate is a book you lend. No main. <code class='inline'>src/lib.rs</code> is page one.",
"Every house is locked at first. Each door needs a <code class='inline'>pub</code> key. <code class='inline'>pub fn</code> means come in. No <code class='inline'>pub</code> means family only.",
"Careful: a <code class='inline'>pub</code> room inside a private <code class='inline'>mod</code> is still locked from the street. You need <code class='inline'>pub</code> on <i>every</i> door.",
"<code class='inline'>use</code> is a short name for a long address. <code class='inline'>use crate::garden::veg::Carrot</code> means you just write <code class='inline'>Carrot</code> after that."
],
analogy:[["One book / a box of books / a chapter","=","crate / package / module"],["A locked house — a key at every door","=","private by default, pub opens"],["A shiny key inside a locked house","=","pub mod inside a private mod — still locked"],["A nickname for a long address","=","use — the shortcut"]],
coach:"Big house? No fear. Rename a door. Move a wall. The house still stands. Rust checks each wall as you build. A bad wall stops the build — before anyone lives in it.",
widget:{type:"modulemap"},
code:{
copy:`// One file version (runs as-is). Real projects split each mod
// into its own file: mod garden; finds src/garden.rs.
mod garden {
    pub mod veg { // veg = vegetables room
        pub struct Carrot;
    }
}
use crate::garden::veg::Carrot; // shortcut, Carrot = orange veg

// everything PRIVATE by default! need pub key at each door.
mod front { // private front room
    pub mod hosting { // public inside private = still not seen!
        pub fn add() { println!("welcome!"); }
    }
}
fn main() {
    let _c = Carrot;
    // front::hosting::add(); // ERROR: front is private, locked door
    println!("ok");
}`,
lines:[
["// One file version (runs as-is). Real projects split each mod","one file now, many files later"],
["mod garden {","a chapter named garden"],
["    pub mod veg { // veg = vegetables room","an open room inside it"],
["        pub struct Carrot;","an open toy inside the room"],
["use crate::garden::veg::Carrot;","the shortcut — just write Carrot now"],
["mod front { // private front room","no pub — this door is locked"],
["    pub mod hosting { // public inside private = still not seen!","a shiny key inside a locked house"],
["    let _c = Carrot;","the shortcut works"],
["    // front::hosting::add(); // ERROR: front is private, locked door","the locked door refuses — this line would not build"],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"The three 'pub' powers:",b:"<code class='inline'>pub</code> = all may use it. <code class='inline'>pub(crate)</code> = only this house. <code class='inline'>pub(super)</code> = only the parent room. Start locked. Open a door only when someone outside knocks."},
why:{kick:"how teams use this in real code",
h:"Big projects are houses of rooms. The three pub levels keep the outside small and clean.",
points:[
"<b>pub(crate) does the daily work.</b> Helpers that are not public stay hidden. Other crates cannot use them.",
"<b>Paths tell the story.</b> crate::garden::veg::Carrot shows where it lives. One look, no messy imports.",
"<b>lib.rs holds shared code.</b> main.rs stays thin and uses it. That split is why the two files exist."
]},
done:"Organizes a program into modules"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"vecs", nav:"Vectors",
title:"Vectors — <em>a shelf of items</em>",
lead:"One box holds one thing. A Vec is a shelf that holds many in a row and can grow.",
words:[
"You start with <code>Vec::new()</code> — empty. Then you <code>push</code> one value in.",
"You read safe with <code>get(0)</code>. It gives <code>Some(&value)</code>. Or <code>None</code> if the spot is empty.",
"You read fast with <code>v[0]</code>. But a missing spot crashes the program.",
"You add many at once with <code>extend([2,3])</code>. And you can ask for <code>len</code> and <code>capacity</code>.",
"<code>len</code> is how many items now. <code>capacity</code> is how much room is ready."
],
widget:{type:"shelf"},
code:{
copy:`fn main() {
    let mut v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new(); // v = [] len 0 cap 0
    v.push(1); // v = [1] len 1
    v.push(2); // v = [1, 2] len 2
    v.extend([3, 4]); // v = [1, 2, 3, 4] len 4 — add many at once
    println!("len {} cap {}", v.len(), v.capacity()); // shows: len 4 cap 4
    println!("{:?}", v.get(0)); // shows: Some(&1) — safe, no stop
    println!("{:?}", v.get(10)); // shows: None — missing, no stop
    println!("{}", v[0]); // shows: 1 — fast, but v[10] would stop
}`,
lines:[
["fn main() {","you open the program door"],
["    let mut v: Vec<i32> = Vec::new();","v = [] empty, len 0 cap 0"],
["    v.push(1);","push 1 → v = [1] len 1"],
["    v.push(2);","push 2 → v = [1, 2] len 2"],
["    v.extend([3, 4]);","extend adds 3,4 → v = [1,2,3,4] len 4"],
["    println!(\"len {} cap {}\", v.len(), v.capacity());","shows: len 4 cap 4 — count vs room"],
["    println!(\"{:?}\", v.get(0));","shows: Some(&1) — safe look"],
["    println!(\"{:?}\", v.get(10));","shows: None — out of range, no crash"],
["    println!(\"{}\", v[0]);","shows: 1 — fast index, but [10] would stop"],
["}","door closes"]
]},
tip:{h:"Which one to use?",b:"Not sure the spot is full? Use <code>get(i)</code> — it gives <code>None</code>. Sure it is full? Use <code>v[i]</code>. <code>len</code> is how many you have. <code>capacity</code> is ready room."},
done:"Uses Vecs"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"hashmap", nav:"HashMap",
title:"HashMap — <em>the label maker</em>",
lead:"A Vec finds things by spot number. A HashMap finds things by label — at once.",
words:[
"A Vec keeps items in a line. You find them by number. A <b>HashMap</b> keeps items with <b>labels</b>. You name each item. Later you say the name — and get it back at once.",
"Think of jars with labels: you write \"sugar\" on a jar and fill it. To find sugar, you do not open every jar. You read the labels and grab it.",
"In code: <code class='inline'>map.insert(\"sugar\", 2);</code> puts 2 in the jar labeled sugar. <code class='inline'>map.get(\"sugar\")</code> gives <code class='inline'>Some(&2)</code>. No such label? You get <code class='inline'>None</code>. That is why Option lives here.",
"Use HashMap for \"find it by name\": count words, game points, settings. Use Vec when order matters.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>labels on jars — say the label, get the jar.</i>"
],
analogy:[["Jars with printed labels","=","HashMap<String, i32>"],["Saying the label out loud","=","map.get(\"sugar\")"],["A jar with no label on it","=","None (Option)"]],
widget:{type:"labelmaker"},
code:{
copy:`let mut scores = HashMap::new();
scores.insert("rusty", 10);    // stick label "rusty" -> 10
scores.insert("frost", 8);
let got = scores.get("rusty"); // Some(&10) — label found!`,
lines:[
["let mut scores = HashMap::new();","an empty wall of jars"],
["scores.insert(\"rusty\", 10);","print label rusty, stick on jar, fill with 10"],
["scores.insert(\"frost\", 8);","another jar"],
["let got = scores.get(\"rusty\");","say the label — instant answer, Some(&10)"]
]},
tip:{h:"Remember the label:",b:"HashMap means \"jars with labels\". Option is the answer when a label is missing. The two almost always come together."},
done:"Uses HashMaps"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"errors", nav:"Errors",
title:"Result & errors — <em>the safe way to fail</em>",
lead:"Things fail in real life: files go missing, networks drop. Rust handles failure with Result.",
words:[
"<b>Result</b> is like Option, but with a reason. <code class='inline'>Ok(value)</code> — it worked! <code class='inline'>Err(problem)</code> — it failed, here is why.",
"Rust <b>makes</b> you look at failure. A function that can fail gives you a Result. You must choose what to do with it. You cannot quietly ignore it.",
"<code class='inline'>unwrap()</code> means \"I am sure this works, give me the value\". If it is really an Err, the program <b>crashes</b> with a message. Fine for practice. Bad for real programs. <code class='inline'>match</code> is the safe way.",
"Try the conveyor: read files. Some are there. Some are not. See what unwrap does when it is wrong.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>Result is Ok or Err — look at both, match is safe, unwrap can crash.</i>"
],
widget:{type:"resultplay"},
code:{
copy:`let result = std::fs::read_to_string("notes.txt");
match result {
    Ok(text)   => println!("content: {}", text),
    Err(e)     => println!("oops: {}", e),
}
// let text = result.unwrap(); // CRASH if Err!`,
lines:[
["let result = std::fs::read_to_string(\"notes.txt\");","try to read the file — may fail"],
["match result {","open the result box"],
["    Ok(text)   => println!(\"content: {}\", text),","worked! → print the text"],
["    Err(e)     => println!(\"oops: {}\", e),","failed! → print the reason"],
["}","handled safely"],
["// let text = result.unwrap();","unwrap = trust me... CRASH if it's Err!"]
]},
tip:{h:"unwrap or match:",b:"<code class='inline'>unwrap</code> = fast (crashes on failure). <code class='inline'>match</code> = safe (handles both). For practice, unwrap is fine. For real programs, handle your errors."},
done:"Understands Result & errors"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"files", nav:"Files",
title:"Files — <em>making the computer remember</em>",
lead:"Programs forget all when they close. Files help them remember — and help two programs talk.",
words:[
"<code class='inline'>fs::write(\"name.txt\", \"content\")</code> makes a file on disk (or writes over it). <code class='inline'>fs::read_to_string(\"name.txt\")</code> reads it back. <code class='inline'>fs::remove_file</code> deletes it.",
"Files are notes on the computer's desk. They stay after your program ends. Next run, it can read them again.",
"Reading can fail (no file). So read gives you a <code class='inline'>Result</code> — that is why the last lesson exists.",
"Try it: write a note, read it, delete it. Real programs use these same three moves every day."
],
widget:{type:"fileplay"},
code:{
copy:`fs::write("note.txt", "hello file!").ok();
let text = fs::read_to_string("note.txt")
    .expect("file should exist");
println!("{}", text);
fs::remove_file("note.txt").ok();`,
lines:[
["fs::write(\"note.txt\", \"hello file!\").ok();","save a note to disk"],
["let text = fs::read_to_string(\"note.txt\")","open the note back up"],
["    .expect(\"file should exist\");","...or crash with this message"],
["println!(\"{}\", text);","print the note"],
["fs::remove_file(\"note.txt\").ok();","delete the note"]
]},
tip:{h:"expect = unwrap with your words:",b:"<code class='inline'>expect(\"message\")</code> is <code class='inline'>unwrap()</code>, but on crash YOUR words show — much easier to find the bug. <code class='inline'>.ok()</code> means \"on fail, skip it\"."},
done:"Reads & writes files"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"generics", nav:"Generics",
title:"Generics — <em>one mold, many doughs</em>",
lead:"Write the code once. Use it with any kind.",
words:[
"A <b>generic</b> is a mold: one shape, filled with what you like. <code class='inline'>Vec&lt;i32&gt;</code> and <code class='inline'>Vec&lt;String&gt;</code> are the same mold with a different fill. The Vec code was written once.",
"The angle brackets hold the kind: <code class='inline'>struct Rectangle&lt;T&gt; { width: T, height: T }</code> means \"width and height are some kind T\". Later you pick: <code class='inline'>Rectangle&lt;i32&gt;</code>, <code class='inline'>Rectangle&lt;f64&gt;</code> — all from one mold.",
"Why? You write the logic once. Sort, store, compare — the steps are the same, no matter the items. And Rust checks each fill <i>before</i> your program runs.",
"Two spots: <code class='inline'>struct</code>/<code class='inline'>fn</code> with <code class='inline'>&lt;T&gt;</code> is where you make the mold. Each real kind you write is a fill. Same shape, many fills.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>one mold, many fills — same code, any kind.</i>"
],
analogy:[["A cookie mold (one shape)","=","struct Rectangle<T>"],["Cookie dough / chocolate dough","=","Rectangle<i32> / Rectangle<String>"],["One mold takes any fill","=","Rust checks each fill before it runs"]],
widget:{type:"mold"},
code:{
copy:`struct Rectangle<T> { width: T, height: T }   // the mold
let a = Rectangle { width: 5, height: 3 };     // filled with numbers
let b = Rectangle { width: "wide", height: "tall" }; // filled with text`,
lines:[
["struct Rectangle<T> { width: T, height: T }","the mold — T is \"some type, decided later\""],
["let a = Rectangle { width: 5, height: 3 };","filling 1: numbers"],
["let b = Rectangle { width: \"wide\", height: \"tall\" };","filling 2: text — same mold, no new code"]
]},
tip:{h:"<T> = \"some kind, pick later\":",b:"Angle brackets always mean a mold. The kind inside is the fill. Same shape, many fills — that is generics."},
done:"Uses generics"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"traits", nav:"Traits",
title:"Traits — <em>the skill cards</em>",
lead:"Traits answer: what can this thing DO? One shared skill, held by many kinds.",
words:[
"A Dog can bark. A Cat can meow. Different kinds, but both can do things. A <b>trait</b> lists skills: \"anything with this card can do these things\".",
"You have used traits since lesson one! <code class='inline'>println!</code> only prints things with the <b>Display</b> card — the skill to turn into text. Numbers have it. String has it. <code class='inline'>#[derive(Debug)]</code> on a struct says: \"give my struct the skill to print with {:?}\".",
"To promise: <code class='inline'>trait Describe { fn describe(&self) -> String; }</code> — just the promise, no code. To keep the promise: <code class='inline'>impl Describe for Dog { ... }</code> — the real skill, written for one kind.",
"The win: a function that takes <code class='inline'>impl Describe</code> does not care. Dog or Cat — any kind with the card works. No family trees needed.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a trait is a skill card — any kind with the card can do these things.</i>"
],
analogy:[["Skill cards in a game (Bark, Meow)","=","Traits"],["Filling in a card for your animal","=","impl Bark for Dog"],["\"Anything with the Display card prints\"","=","println! takes Display kinds"]],
widget:{type:"ability"},
code:{
copy:`trait Describe {                       // the promise
    fn describe(&self) -> String;
}
impl Describe for Dog {                // Dog's version
    fn describe(&self) -> String { "woof".to_string() }
}
impl Describe for Cat {                // Cat's version
    fn describe(&self) -> String { "meow".to_string() }
}
fn announce<T: Describe>(x: T) {       // any Describe works
    println!("{}", x.describe());
}`,
lines:[
["trait Describe {","the promise: anything with this card can describe itself"],
["    fn describe(&self) -> String;","the required ability — just the signature"],
["impl Describe for Dog {","filling in the card for Dog"],
["    fn describe(&self) -> String { \"a good boy\".to_string() }","Dog's actual answer"],
["impl Describe for Cat {","filling in the same card for Cat"],
["fn announce<T: Describe>(x: T) {","T: Describe = \"any type that holds the card\""],
["    println!(\"{}\", x.describe());","works for Dog AND Cat — same function"]
]},
tip:{h:"Trait = what it CAN do:",b:"struct says what a thing IS. impl says how it acts. trait says what any kind must do to join a group. Read <code class='inline'>T: Describe</code> as \"T has the skill\"."},
done:"Uses traits"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"lifetimes", nav:"Lifetimes",
title:"Lifetimes — <em>how long the water stays</em>",
lead:"Borrowing says: you may look, not take. Lifetimes answer next: how long may you look?",
words:[
"Every value has a <b>lifetime</b> — the part of the code where it stays alive. Picture each pool with a timer: it starts when you make the pool. It ends when the pool drains.",
"A look can only live <i>while the pool still has water</i>. Keep looking after it drained? Rust stops the program before it runs: <b>borrowed value does not live long enough</b>.",
"You almost never write lifetimes yourself — <code class='inline'>'a</code> is just a name. But you will read them: <code class='inline'>fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str</code> says \"the answer lives as long as the questions\". The <code class='inline'>'a</code> links all three. The answer cannot outlive what it came from.",
"Try the lifeguard toy below: a look inside the zone is fine. A look outside the zone — where the water is gone — is the error real Rust shows you.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a look lives only while the water is there.</i>"
],
analogy:[["A pool of water (alive only while the tap runs)","=","A value's lifetime"],["Carrying a cup out after the pool drained","=","A reference that outlives its value"],["The lifeguard enforcing the rule","=","The borrow checker"]],
widget:{type:"lifeguard"},
code:{
copy:`fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {
    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }
}
// 'a = "as long as the inputs"`,
lines:[
["fn longest<'a>(x: &'a str, y: &'a str) -> &'a str {","declare: one lifetime 'a shared by x, y and the answer"],
["    if x.len() > y.len() { x } else { y }","pick the longer one"],
["}","the answer can't outlive x or y — 'a says so"],
["// 'a = \"as long as the inputs\"","the one-sentence summary"]
]},
tip:{h:"The one-line rule:",b:"A look may only live while the value is still alive. That is all of lifetimes — the rest is Rust helping you keep that promise."},
done:"Understands lifetimes"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"tests", nav:"Automated tests",
title:"Testing — <em>the safety net</em>",
lead:"A net under a walker: you may fall as much as you want. The net catches you.",
words:[
"A test is a small program that checks one promise. Like this: <i>you feed it 5 — does it really answer 10?</i>",
"You write <code class='inline'>#[test]</code> over a function. You run <code class='inline'>cargo test</code>. A green check means yes.",
"Three lines start every test room. <code class='inline'>#[cfg(test)]</code> means this room is only there when testing. <code class='inline'>mod tests</code> is the test room. Inside sit small functions full of <code class='inline'>assert_eq!</code>.",
"Why? Because <b>code changes</b>. One day you rewrite <code class='inline'>double()</code> in a new style. The small net warns at once: <i>you broke the 5→10 promise</i>.",
"Two more tools teams use daily. <code class='inline'>#[should_panic(expected = \"...\")]</code> passes only when the code stops with those words. And <code class='inline'>cargo test it_works</code> runs just one test by name.",
"<b>Rule:</b> <code class='inline'>assert_eq!</code> needs <code class='inline'>PartialEq + Debug</code>. Add <code class='inline'>#[derive(PartialEq, Debug)]</code> to your structs. Then the net can compare them."
],
analogy:[["A safety net","=","assert_eq! catching a broken promise"],["A practice run before the show","=","cargo test"],["A list of promise checks","=","one #[test] function each"]],
coach:"Tests look like extra lines. They are not. They are the reason you can change code fast. Pros write them first, then the code. Try that order once — it feels good.",
widget:{type:"testplay"},
code:{
copy:`#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn double_works() {
        assert_eq!(double(5), 10);
    }
}`,
lines:[
["#[cfg(test)]","'build this room only during cargo test'"],
["mod tests {","the test room"],
["    use super::*;","bring the real code in from the parent"],
["    #[test]","'this function is a promise'"],
["    fn double_works() {","a test that checks one behavior"],
["        assert_eq!(double(5), 10);","'5 in must give 10 out' — if not, the net shakes"],
["    }",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Test names tell stories:",b:"Name tests like short stories: <code class='inline'>double_works</code>, <code class='inline'>empty_string_adds_nothing</code>. When one fails, <code class='inline'>cargo test</code> shows the name. You know at once which promise broke."},
why:{kick:"why teams test first",
h:"Write the test first. Then make the code keep the promise. That order has a name: TDD.",
points:[
"<b>Tests are notes that cannot lie.</b> 'What happens for \"--file x\"?' The answer is not in a comment. It is an assert_eq! that runs.",
"<b>Change code with no fear.</b> New style, same answers — the tests say 'still good'.",
"<b>Read left to right.</b> assert_eq!(actual, wanted) is the style all use. 'double(5) must be 10'."
]},
done:"Writes tests that check the code"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"minigrep", nav:"minigrep — a mini tool",
title:"minigrep — <em>your first real tool</em>",
lead:"Result, files, and iterators meet here. A small grep that searches a file.",
words:[
"grep hunts for a word in a file. It prints each line with the word. We build <b>minigrep</b> — the small version — with <code class='inline'>std</code> only. No extra crates.",
"First, the program must read the command line. The words after your program live in <code class='inline'>env::args()</code>. We <code class='inline'>collect()</code> them into a Vec. Two dashes split cargo's words from yours: <code class='inline'>cargo run -- needle poem.txt</code>.",
"Then <code class='inline'>fs::read_to_string</code> reads the whole file into one big String. No file? No crash — a <b>Result</b> comes back. We open it with care: a clear message, not a crash.",
"Then the key line: <code class='inline'>contents.lines().filter(|line| line.contains(query))</code>. Three steps on one belt. Out come the lines with the word. No for-loop.",
"The pro version splits the work. <code class='inline'>Config::build</code> reads the args and can fail in a nice way. <code class='inline'>run()</code> does the job and gives back a <code class='inline'>Result</code>. Errors go to <code class='inline'>eprintln!</code> plus <code class='inline'>process::exit(1)</code>. Red text, no crash."
],
analogy:[["A librarian asked 'find lines with NEEDLE'","=","minigrep query + file"],["Reading the whole book first","=","contents.lines() — read the file"],["Keeping only matching lines","=",".filter(|line| …)"]],
coach:"Reading a whole file into memory is fine for a first tool. Big real grep reads piece by piece. The Book's own minigrep also reads it all at once. Small first, fast later.",
widget:{type:"resultplay"},
code:{
copy:`let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();
let query = &args[1];              // the word
let file = &args[2];               // the file

let contents = fs::read_to_string(file)
    .expect("could not read that file");

for line in contents.lines().filter(|l| l.contains(query)) {
    println!("{}", line);
}`,
lines:[
["let args: Vec<String> = env::args().collect();","the whole command line, one item per word"],
["let query = &args[1];","word #1 after the program name — borrow, don't copy"],
["let file = &args[2];","file path #2 — borrow"],
["let contents = fs::read_to_string(file)","open the file… Result&lt;string, Error&gt; comes back"],
["    .expect(\"could not read that file\");","if the box is Err, print this message and stop"],
["for line in contents.lines().filter(|l| l.contains(query))","walk only the lines that contain the word"],
["    println!(\"{}\", line);","print each winner"],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Why args[1] not args[0] — plus one setting:",b:"args[0] is always your own program name. Real words start at 1. Count them: <code class='inline'>minigrep NEEDLE poem.txt</code> → args[1] is NEEDLE, args[2] is poem.txt. And for BIG and small letters, set it outside the command: <code class='inline'>IGNORE_CASE=1 cargo run -- needle poem.txt</code>. The program reads it with <code class='inline'>env::var(\"IGNORE_CASE\").is_ok()</code>."},
why:{kick:"why this project matters",
h:"minigrep is the Book's 'it all clicks here' project. Many learners say the same: here the language starts to make sense.",
points:[
"<b>The borrow checker teaches.</b> The config change (query: &str) makes you meet lifetimes. The compiler walks you through.",
"<b>Settings live outside the command.</b> Real tools (ripgrep, git) read the air around them, not just flags.",
"<b>It ships.</b> The Book ends with 'run the tests you wrote'. This small tool is already safe to share."
]},
done:"Built a real CLI tool that reads files"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"closures", nav:"Closures & iterators",
title:"Closures — <em>small helpers with no name</em>",
lead:"A closure is a function with no name. It can use the values near it.",
words:[
"A <b>normal function</b> only sees what you hand it: <code class='inline'>feed(water, hay)</code>. It gets the inputs. Nothing else. It forgets all when done.",
"A <b>closure</b> also sees what sits near it. <code class='inline'>|| donut.clone()</code> — the pipes are hands, they grab, done. It <b>uses</b> the values around it. That is called <b>capture</b>.",
"Two shapes: <code class='inline'>|x| x + 5</code> takes x and adds 5. Empty pipes <code class='inline'>|| 5</code> take nothing — always 5.",
"The word <code class='inline'>move</code> changes the deal. <code class='inline'>move || …</code> means 'take these values with you'. It gives them to the new thread for good."
],
analogy:[["A named recipe — hand it all","=","calling a normal function"],["A helper that uses what is near","=","a closure using its space"],["Giving values to a new thread","=","move || gives ownership away"]],
coach:"Inside each closure, Rust builds a small hidden box. It holds what the closure took. Three kinds: <b>Fn</b> reads, <b>FnMut</b> changes, <b>FnOnce</b> eats. First use picks the kind. It never changes.",
widget:{type:"conveyor"},
code:{
copy:`let offset = 10;
let add_offset = |x: i32| x + offset;   // captures offset

let mut inventory = vec!["hay", "carrot"];
let push_feed = || { inventory.push("donut"); }; // FnMut

let heavy = String::from("box");
std::thread::spawn(move || { drop(heavy); })    // move`,
lines:[
["let offset = 10;","the ingredient sitting in the pocket"],
["let add_offset = |x: i32| x + offset;","a closure that borrows offset without being told"],
["let mut inventory = vec![…];","a Vec that might get fed"],
["let push_feed = || { inventory.push(\"donut\"); };","this closure needs to change it → FnMut"],
["let heavy = String::from(\"box\");","a String destined for another thread"],
["std::thread::spawn(move || { drop(heavy); })","move steals heavy for the new thread's cart"],
["",""]
]},
tip:{h:"The three Fn kinds:",b:"<code class='inline'>Fn</code> reads (borrows). <code class='inline'>FnMut</code> changes (borrows to change). <code class='inline'>FnOnce</code> eats (takes). First use picks the kind. It never changes."},
why:{kick:"why iterators need them",
h:"Iterators are belts. Closures are the hands on the belt. Made for each other.",
points:[
"<b>.filter(|x| …), .map(|x| …), .unwrap_or_else(|| …)</b> are all closures. Most iterator tools take them.",
"<b>Less passing around.</b> No long input lists. The closure just uses what is near.",
"<b>move feeds threads.</b> New thread, same values. The checker stays calm."
]},
done:"Writes closures and moves them between threads"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"iterators", nav:"Iterators & Closures",
title:"Iterators & closures — <em>the conveyor belt</em>",
lead:"The most Rust-like code you will write: say what you want. Let the belt walk.",
words:[
"An <b>iterator</b> is a belt running over your shelf. It does not move until you ask. That laziness is on purpose: the belt only does the work you really need.",
"<b>Closures</b> are small functions with no name: <code class='inline'>|x| x * 2</code> means \"take x, give back x doubled\". The pipes <code class='inline'>| |</code> are hands that catch each item.",
"The classic chain: <code class='inline'>.iter()</code> puts items on the belt → <code class='inline'>.map(|x| x * 2)</code> changes each → <code class='inline'>.filter(|x| x > 4)</code> drops the small ones → <code class='inline'>.collect()</code> packs what is left into a new Vec.",
"A for-loop walks and pushes by hand. The belt version says <i>what</i> you want, not <i>how</i> to walk. Real Rust code looks like this.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>map changes each item. Filter keeps some. Collect packs the box.</i>"
],
analogy:[["A factory conveyor belt","=","An iterator"],["A worker that catches and transforms each item","=","A closure |x| x * 2"],["Dumping the result into a new box","=",".collect()"]],
widget:{type:"conveyor"},
code:{
copy:`let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
let big = nums.iter()
    .map(|x| x * 2)        // every item doubled
    .filter(|x| x > 4)     // keep only the big ones
    .collect::<Vec<_>>();  // pour the rest into a new Vec
// big = [6, 8, 10]`,
lines:[
["let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4, 5];","five items on the loading dock"],
["let big = nums.iter()","put them on the belt"],
["    .map(|x| x * 2)","each item caught and doubled"],
["    .filter(|x| x > 4)","drop the ones that aren't bigger than 4"],
["    .collect::<Vec<_>>();","pour the survivors into a fresh Vec"],
["// big = [6, 8, 10]","the belt's final answer"]
]},
tip:{h:"Read |x| as \"hands take x\":",b:"A closure is a function with no name, given to something else. The belt calls it once per item. <code class='inline'>|x| x * 2</code> — hands take, body doubles."},
done:"Uses iterators & closures"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"crates", nav:"The crate ecosystem",
title:"The crate store — <em>borrow superpowers from others</em>",
lead:"You can build all from zero. Or use 150,000 ready-made boxes made by others.",
words:[
"You learned modules (rooms in YOUR house) in the general track. A <b>crate</b> is someone else's box of code: a ready library, shared with the world. <b>crates.io</b> is the free shelf with 150,000+ crates. <code class='inline'>cargo add</code> is the one command that takes one.",
"Boxes you will meet soon: <b>serde</b> (struct to JSON and back — the most used crate). <b>anyhow</b> (easy errors: write <code class='inline'>?</code>, skip error kinds). <b>clap</b> (reads words like <code class='inline'>--name Bob</code>). <b>tokio</b> (the async kitchen, from your async lesson).",
"One command does all. <code class='inline'>cargo add serde</code> finds the newest version. It loads it down. It writes it in your list (Cargo.toml). Then one <code class='inline'>use</code> line opens their door. That is the whole trick.",
"Good Rust users check first: is it <b>used by many</b> (loads)? Is it <b>fresh</b> (new version)? The Rust group takes this seriously — that is why planes and cars trust it.",
"And when you are ready: <code class='inline'>cargo publish</code> puts YOUR box on the shelf. Your crate, your name, used by someone far away.",
"🔮 <b>Guess before you type:</b> what does <code class='inline'>cargo add serde</code> do? (Hint: find, load, write the list — all alone.) Then try the buttons.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a crate is someone else's box. Cargo add takes it. crates.io is the free shelf.</i>"
],
analogy:[["Taking a box from a neighbor","=","Adding a crate"],["The free shelf of 150,000 boxes","=","crates.io"],["serde, anyhow, clap, tokio","=","The boxes all use"]],
widget:{type:"cargoplay"},
code:{
copy:`# Cargo.toml — the shopping list
[dependencies]
serde = { version = "1", features = ["derive"] }
anyhow = "1"

# main.rs — then use their doors!
fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let data = serde_json::to_string_pretty(&["rust", "from", "zero"])?;
    println!("{data}");
    Ok(())
}`,
lines:[
["[dependencies]","the shopping list starts here"],
["serde = { version = \"1\", features = [\"derive\"] }","cargo add wrote this line for you"],
["anyhow = \"1\"","the friendly error bag"],
["fn main() -> anyhow::Result<()> {","any errors just ride along with ?"],
["    let data = serde_json::to_string_pretty(&[\"rust\", \"from\", \"zero\"])?;","their function, your data"],
["    println!(\"{data}\");","pretty JSON out"],
["    Ok(())","all good"]
]},
tip:{h:"The store rule:",b:"Before you build it yourself, search crates.io once. If a much-used crate exists, take it. Your job is the glue — not building twice what exists."},
done:"Can add and use a crate"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"smartp", nav:"Smart pointers",
title:"Smart pointers — <em>what owns the value now?</em>",
lead:"Plain boxes hold values. Smart pointers hold values — and clean up by themselves.",
words:[
"Two memories. The <b>stack</b> is fast and small. The <b>heap</b> is big and slow to set up. A Box puts the value on the heap.",
"<code class='inline'>Box&lt;T&gt;</code> holds one heap box. One owner only — when the Box ends, the value ends. Good when the kind is big, or its size is not fixed.",
"<code class='inline'>Rc&lt;T&gt;</code> lets one value have many owners. Each <code class='inline'>Rc::clone</code> adds 1 to the count. Last one out turns off the light: the value ends at 0. That is reference-counting.",
"<code class='inline'>RefCell&lt;T&gt;</code> moves the borrow check to <i>run time</i>. You may borrow more than once. Break the rules? It crashes while running — not while building.",
"Deref means <code class='inline'>*</code> opens the box. <code class='inline'>hi(&m)</code> with a big box just works. Rust changes <code class='inline'>&Box&lt;String&gt;</code> into <code class='inline'>&str</code> for you.",
"Drop means clean up when the value ends. Last made, first cleaned. <code class='inline'>drop(toy)</code> ends it early.",
"One warning: <code class='inline'>Rc</code> can loop. Owners holding each other never reach 0. <code class='inline'>Weak</code> breaks the loop. That is the extra note for graphs.",
"The classic mix: <code class='inline'>Rc&lt;RefCell&lt;T&gt;&gt;</code> — many owners, and each may change it."
],
analogy:[["One book lent to many readers, with a count list","=","Rc&lt;T> — many owners"],["One locked box, one key","=","Box&lt;T> — one owner"],["Rules checked late, while running","=","RefCell&lt;T>"]],
coach:"One rule beats all: each value has one true owner somewhere. Box is the normal pick. The rest are special tools for rare jobs. Use <code>Rc&lt;RefCell&lt;T>></code> only when one value truly has owners who must all change it.",
widget:{type:"smartptr"},
code:{
copy:`let note = Box::new("hi");   // heap
println!("The note says: {note}");

let shared = Rc::new("hay");
let second = Rc::clone(&shared);  // +1 on the sheet`,
lines:[
["let note = Box::new(\"hi\");","a box on the heap, with \"hi\" inside"],
["println!(\"The note says: {note}\");","open the box — Rust opens it for you"],
["let shared = Rc::new(\"hay\");","reference-counted — single shared string"],
["let second = Rc::clone(&shared);","+1 on the sign-up sheet — same string, two owners"],
["",""],
]},
tip:{h:"Rc::clone is cheap:",b:"<code class='inline'>Rc::clone(&x)</code> adds 1 to the count. It does not copy the data. <code class='inline'>x.clone()</code> may copy all — slow. So write the Rc form: <code class='inline'>Rc::clone(&x)</code>."},
why:{kick:"where you meet them in real code",
h:"Languages with trash trucks never think of owners. Smart pointers give you that — with no extra cost, no truck.",
points:[
"<b>Box is the normal one.</b> A tree node holds children — children live on the heap. Box is how.",
"<b>Rc + RefCell is strong.</b> Use it little. It cannot stop all bugs the way Send/Sync stop data races.",
"<b>Arc&lt;Mutex&lt;T>> is the threads answer.</b> Arc = Rc that threads may share. Mutex = a lock. Together they hold state across threads."
]},
done:"Uses Box and Rc in real code"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"threads", nav:"Threads",
title:"Threads — <em>more chefs in the kitchen</em>",
lead:"One chef is one thread. Two chefs, two threads. They cook at the same time.",
words:[
"<b>std::thread::spawn</b> hires a new chef. The new chef cooks its own dish. The main chef keeps cooking too. The recipe you give must <b>move</b>. The new chef owns its own food.",
"Chefs can share one board — but only one cuts at a time. The board has a <code class='inline'>Mutex&lt;T&gt;</code> lock. It says \"this chef cuts now\". Chef B waits its turn.",
"Why threads? New chips have many cores — real chefs. One dish at a time wastes nine of ten chefs.",
"The other trick is messages: the main chef shouts down the hall: <code class='inline'>tx.send(hay)</code>. The helper's mailbox brings it later with <code class='inline'>rx.recv()</code>. No shared board needed."
],
analogy:[["Chefs cooking at the same time","=","threads"],["One lock on the board","=","Mutex&lt;T> — one at a time"],["A note in the mailbox","=","channel send/recv"]],
coach:"Sharing is safe with a lock. Lock it. Use the value. Unlock. Rust unlocks for you at the end of the room.",
widget:{type:"threadplay"},
code:{
copy:`use std::thread;

let th = thread::spawn(|| {
    for i in 1..=3 { println!("helper {i}"); }
});
for i in 1..=3 { println!("main   {i}"); }
th.join().unwrap();   // wait for the helper`,
lines:[
["use std::thread;","pull the threading kitchen into scope"],
["let th = thread::spawn(|| {","new thread, new chef; closure must own its food"],
["    for i in 1..=3 { println!(\"helper {i}\"); }","helper's own loop"],
["});","new thread started"],
["for i in 1..=3 { println!(\"main   {i}\"); }","the main thread cooks in parallel"],
["th.join().unwrap();","main waits for the helper to finish — unwrap catches a panic"],
["",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Threads cost — and where you join matters:",b:"Each thread gets its own stack (8 MB). Ten threads are fine. Ten <i>thousand</i> are not. Join <i>after</i> the work: both run together. Join <i>before</i> the work: you just wait, no speed."},
why:{kick:"where you meet threads in real code",
h:"Browsers, games, servers — all use threads. Rust makes them safe: no data races, no extra cost.",
points:[
"<b>Arc&lt;Mutex&lt;T>> does the daily work.</b> Arc is Rc that threads may share. Mutex is RefCell with a lock. That is the map.",
"<b>Channels first.</b> Many Go users start with channels. Rust has both. Channels first; shared state when the story is cleaner.",
"<b>Data races do not build.</b> Share &mut across threads? Rust says no. C++ says yes — and crashes at night.",
"<b>Send + Sync are passports.</b> Send may travel to a new thread. Sync may be shared with &. Rc has no passport. That is why Arc exists."
]},
done:"Spawns threads and sends messages"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"asyncr", nav:"Async & await",
title:"Async — <em>the chef with many whistles</em>",
lead:"Threads hire more chefs. Async gives ONE chef more timers. Start the rice, start the soup, run back when a whistle blows.",
words:[
"One chef, one pot at a time: 3 minutes on soup, then start the rice. Wasteful — the soup just sits there while you wait.",
"<b>Async</b> is a timer per pot. <code class='inline'>.await</code> sets a whistle. The chef starts the soup. Starts the rice. Then runs back to the pot that whistles first. One chef, many pots at once.",
"Rust needs a <b>runtime</b> to hear the whistles: a small engine that sleeps and wakes. <b>tokio</b> is the most used one. <code class='inline'>#[tokio::main]</code> is the front door.",
"The big rule: an <code class='inline'>async fn</code> does NOT run when you call it. It gives back a future. You must <code class='inline'>.await</code> it. Call = order the food. Await = sit down and eat."
],
analogy:[["Hiring more chefs","=","threads — at the same time"],["One chef, many timers","=","async — take turns"],["Standing and staring at one pot","=","blocking code"],["Ordering food (not eating yet)","=","calling async fn gives a Future"]],
coach:"Async fixes <i>waiting</i>, not <i>working</i>. One thread can hold ten thousand waiting lines at almost no cost. Ten thousand threads would each need a big stack. One chef, many whistles.",
widget:{type:"asyncplay"},
code:{
copy:`use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() {
    let rice = sleep_boil("rice", 2);
    let soup = sleep_boil("soup", 3);
    rice.await;   // whistle blows after 2s
    soup.await;   // whistle blows after 3s
}

async fn sleep_boil(name: &str, secs: u64) {
    sleep(Duration::from_secs(secs)).await;
    println!("{name} is done!");
}`,
lines:[
["use tokio::time::{sleep, Duration};","the whistle-box: tokio provides the sleep"],
["#[tokio::main]","the front door — tokio runtime boots here"],
["async fn main() {","main itself is async: everything can await"],
["    let rice = sleep_boil(\"rice\", 2);","ordering the rice — NOT cooked yet (a Future)"],
["    let soup = sleep_boil(\"soup\", 3);","ordering the soup — still not cooked"],
["    rice.await;","now the whistle blows — chef returns to the pot"],
["    soup.await;","and then the second pot"],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Why 3 seconds, not 5 — plus the lazy rule:",b:"We ordered both <i>before</i> awaiting either. Order soup, order rice, then sit down. And: a future is lazy. An <code class='inline'>unused future</code> warning means you forgot <code class='inline'>.await</code> — the job never starts."},
why:{kick:"where you meet async in real code",
h:"Browsers, shops, chats — all wait on thousands of small things at once. That is async land.",
points:[
"<b>tokio is the standard.</b> axum, reqwest, most company servers sit on tokio. #[tokio::main] is your door to all of it.",
"<b>.await can wait with a limit.</b> Pot too slow? Cut the whistle. No need to kill the thread.",
"<b>Threads + async are friends.</b> tokio runs futures on a team of threads. Async is the plan. Threads are the workers."
]},
done:"Awaits futures like a real server dev"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"dyn", nav:"Trait objects (dyn)",
title:"dyn Trait — <em>the shape-shifting box</em>",
lead:"Generics print a fresh copy per kind. dyn is ONE box that holds ANY kind.",
words:[
"You met skill cards (traits) and the mold (generics). With generics, Rust <b>prints a fresh copy</b> for each kind you use. Like a printer: new page per animal. Fast. But the kind must be fixed before the program runs.",
"<b>dyn</b> is one box for <b>any</b> kind. <code class='inline'>Box&lt;dyn Speak&gt;</code> can hold a Dog, a Cat, or a Robot. Same box, always one small note (8 bytes). The real animal lives on the heap. The box just says where.",
"The price: the computer must <b>peek in the box</b> before each <code class='inline'>speak()</code> call, to find the real animal. That is <i>dynamic dispatch</i> — a tiny extra check when it runs. Generics check before running. dyn checks while running.",
"When? When <b>one list must hold mixed kinds</b> — a game with ghosts AND goblins, a plugin box, screen shapes. One fixed kind per spot? Stay with generics. That is the normal pick.",
"🔮 <b>Guess before you click:</b> how big is a <code class='inline'>Box&lt;dyn Speak&gt;</code>? One note, always 8 bytes — no matter what is inside. Click an animal. See the same box change shape.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>generics print a fresh copy per kind. dyn is one box for many kinds.</i>"
],
analogy:[["A printer, fresh page per animal","=","Generics — one copy per kind"],["One box holding any animal","=","Box<dyn Speak> — one box, many kinds"],["Peeking in the box first","=","Dynamic dispatch"]],
widget:{type:"dynplay"},
code:{
copy:`trait Speak { fn speak(&self) -> String; }
struct Dog; struct Cat; struct Robot;
impl Speak for Dog { fn speak(&self) -> String { "woof!".into() } }
impl Speak for Cat { fn speak(&self) -> String { "meow!".into() } }
impl Speak for Robot { fn speak(&self) -> String { "beep boop".into() } }

let boxed: Box<dyn Speak> = Box::new(Dog);  // one box…
let boxed: Box<dyn Speak> = Box::new(Cat);  // …any shape
println!("{}", boxed.speak());`,
lines:[
["trait Speak { fn speak(&self) -> String; }","the ability card"],
["struct Dog; struct Cat; struct Robot;","three different types"],
["impl Speak for Dog { … }","three cards filled in"],
["let boxed: Box<dyn Speak> = Box::new(Dog);","one box holds the Dog…"],
["let boxed: Box<dyn Speak> = Box::new(Cat);","…then the same box holds the Cat"],
["println!(\"{}\", boxed.speak());","the box speaks — whatever is inside"]
]},
tip:{h:"The one-line pick:",b:"One fixed kind per spot → generic (fast, normal). One box with a MIX of kinds → dyn (flexible). Most Rust code is generic. dyn shows up when lists must hold many shapes."},
done:"Knows when to reach for dyn"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"pattern", nav:"Patterns & matching",
title:"Patterns — <em>the blueprint the data must fit</em>",
lead:"A pattern is a shape test. You hold a value up to it: does it fit? What comes out?",
words:[
"<code class='inline'>match</code> is the biggest test: <code class='inline'>match value { SHAPE => … , _ => … }</code>. Arms run top to bottom. The first shape that fits wins. Three shape kinds: <b>exact</b> (fits just this), <b>name</b> (fits all, keeps it), <b>_</b> (fits all, drops it).",
"<code class='inline'>if let</code> asks one quick question: <code class='inline'>if let Some(x) = maybe { … }</code> — 'a full box? Then hand me x'. A match with one arm. No else needed.",
"<b>let-else</b> is the strict one: <code class='inline'>let Some(x) = maybe else { return; }</code> — 'open it, or leave'. Best at the top of a function, when None means 'nothing to do'.",
"Patterns also <b>open boxes up</b>: <code class='inline'>let (a, b) = pair;</code> splits pairs and structs into parts."
],
analogy:[["A shape sorter","=","a pattern"],["First shape that fits wins","=","match arms, top to bottom"],["Checking one pocket for a coin","=","if let Some(x)"],["Open it or walk out","=","let-else"]],
coach:"Slow down. The power is not the signs. It is this: some shapes always fit (x, _). Some can fail (Some(x)). A plain let needs one that always fits. If it can fail, use if let. Rust stops a bad let before it runs.",
widget:{type:"gym", q:[
{q:"Which shape always fits AND keeps the value?", hint:"a plain small name",
a:"x (any small name) — it fits all and keeps it. _ drops it.", why:"Small names always fit. The line _ says: fits, but I do not care."},
{q:"I only care about the Some case. What starts the short form?", hint:"'if' plus a friend",
a:"if let — if let Some(v) = maybe { … }", why:"A match with one arm. Best when None just means 'skip'."},
{q:"let Some(v) = x else { return; } — what is this?", hint:"open or leave",
a:"let-else: open it, or leave the function early.", why:"The else runs when the shape fails. It must leave (return/break/crash) — so after it, v is safe."},
{q:"Which shape can fail?", hint:"it may not fit",
a:"Some(v) — the box may be empty.", why:"Such shapes cannot sit in a plain let. The compiler says 'use if let'."},
{q:"How to add one more check to a match arm?", hint:"a guard at the door",
a:"Some(n) if n == 10 => ... — the arm runs only when the check is yes.", why:"Guards filter more. Check fails? Move to the next arm."},
{q:"_ or _x — both drop it. What is different?", hint:"one moves, one does not",
a:"_ never moves. _x moves but hides the warning.", why:"Some(_) peeks, no move. Some(_s) moves into _s."}
]},
code:{
copy:`let maybe = Some(5);

match maybe {
    Some(x) => println!("match: got {x}"),
    None    => println!("match: nothing"),
}

if let Some(x) = maybe { println!("if let: got {x}"); }

let level = 5;
match level {
    1 | 2     => println!("tiny"),
    3..=6     => println!("mid"),
    n if n > 100 => println!("huge {n}"),
    _         => println!("other"),
}

let Some(x) = maybe else {
    println!("nowhere to go — leaving");
    return;
};
println!("let-else guarantees x: {x}");`,
lines:[
["let maybe = Some(5);","a maybe-box with a 5 in it"],
["match maybe {","the full grammar — every case must be covered"],
["    Some(x) => println!(…),","shape: a Some box → grab inner as x"],
["    None    => println!(…),","shape: an empty box → say so"],
["if let Some(x) = maybe { … }","one question: is it Some? then use x"],
["let level = 5;","a plain number to grade"],
["    1 | 2     => println!(…),","shape: 1 or 2 (| means or)"],
["    3..=6     => println!(…),","shape: 3 to 6, end included (..= keeps the end)"],
["    n if n > 100 => println!(…),","shape: any number, but only if the guard holds"],
["    _         => println!(…),","shape: everything else"],
["let Some(x) = maybe else {","unwrap or leave…"],
["    return;","…and return must snap the function shut"],
["};","past this line, x is guaranteed real"],
[""]
]},
tip:{h:"One grammar for all:",b:"<code class='inline'>let (x, y) = pair;</code> opens a pair. The same shapes work in match, if let, let-else, while let, and function inputs."},
why:{kick:"why teams love match",
h:"match replaces long if-else chains. Forget a case? The compiler lights it up.",
points:[
"<b>Full cover stops bugs.</b> .unwrap() on None crashes. match makes you handle None. The compiler knows each enum and waits till all are covered.",
"<b>Open boxes read clean.</b> <code class='inline'>let (a, b) = pair;</code> says what is there. Short and clear.",
"<b>Guards add a check:</b> <code class='inline'>match x { n if n > 10 => …, _ => … }</code>. Check fails? Arm skipped."
]},
done:"Masters match, if let and let-else"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"unsafe", nav:"Unsafe",
title:"Unsafe — <em>the unlocked door</em>",
lead:"Every rule so far, the bodyguard checked. unsafe is the one door the guard leaves open.",
words:[
"The borrow checker is the guard: it checks all before your program runs. <code class='inline'>unsafe { }</code> is a <b>door the guard leaves open</b>. Inside, the rules are <b>your job</b>. Rust stops checking there.",
"Five extra powers, the full list. 1) open a <b>raw pointer</b>. 2) call an <b>unsafe function</b>. 3) read/write a <code class='inline'>static mut</code>. 4) keep an <b>unsafe trait</b> promise. 5) touch a <b>union</b> field. Only these. Small and dull, not dark magic.",
"Why does it exist? To talk to C code. For rare speed wins. And to <b>build the safe parts you already use</b>. Vec and Box are safe outside, unsafe inside: safe door, unchecked engine.",
"The golden rule: <b>wrap unsafe in a safe function</b>. Callers never see the door. Rust's promise: <i>\"unsafe code must be as safe as safe code\"</i>. Keep the block small, wrap it, test it.",
"Real check: <b>95 of 100 Rust programs use zero unsafe</b>. You will write it rarely. You will READ it in famous tools (runtimes, game engines). Now you know the word.",
"🔮 <b>Guess before you click:</b> in safe Rust, can you open a raw pointer? (No promises, no opening.) Try it — then see inside the unsafe block.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>unsafe is the open door — keep it small, wrap it safe.</i>"
],
analogy:[["The guard at the door","=","Safe Rust"],["Walking through alone","=","The unsafe block"],["Safe door, unchecked engine","=","Vec: safe outside, unsafe inside"]],
widget:{type:"unsafeplay"},
code:{
copy:`let mut num = 5;
let r1 = &num as *const i32;  // a RAW pointer — no promises
unsafe {
    println!("{}", *r1);      // inside: YOU promise it's alive
}
// outside, the guard is back — rules enforced again`,
lines:[
["let mut num = 5;","a normal number"],
["let r1 = &num as *const i32;","a raw pointer — the guard doesn't track it"],
["unsafe {","the door the guard leaves"],
["    println!(\"{}\", *r1);","inside: you promise the pointer is alive"],
["}","guard back — safe rules enforced again"]
]},
tip:{h:"The whole trick:",b:"Keep the unsafe block SMALL. Wrap it in a safe function. Test it. The guard may leave the room — never the house."},
done:"Understands what unsafe really is"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"macros", nav:"Macros",
title:"Macros — <em>the tiny robot writer</em>",
lead:"println! and vec! felt like magic till now. Meet the small robot behind them.",
words:[
"You have used macros since lesson one: <code class='inline'>println!</code>, <code class='inline'>vec!</code>, <code class='inline'>#[derive(Debug)]</code> — all macros. A <b>macro</b> is a small robot that <b>writes Rust code for you</b>, before your program runs.",
"<code class='inline'>vec![4, 2, 9]</code> looks like a function. But no function can do this: the robot sees three numbers and writes three <code class='inline'>push</code> lines. Function? Fixed list. Macro? <b>Writes what fits.</b> That is why 0, 3, or 100 items all work.",
"You teach the robot once with <code class='inline'>macro_rules!</code>: the shape to spot, and the code to write. After that, one short line turns into a full block — with no mistakes.",
"One more power: macros see <b>code, not answers</b>. <code class='inline'>sq!(5)</code> turns into <code class='inline'>5 * 5</code> — the robot copies the question itself. A function only ever gets the answer.",
"🔮 <b>Guess before you expand:</b> <code class='inline'>myvec![4, 2, 9]</code> — how many push lines will the robot write? Guess, press Expand, see it type.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>a macro is a small robot. It writes Rust code before the program runs.</i>"
],
analogy:[["A small robot that writes dull code for you","=","A macro"],["The note the robot follows","=","macro_rules! { shape => code }"],["The robot copying the question itself","=","Macros see code, not just answers"]],
widget:{type:"macroplay"},
code:{
copy:`macro_rules! myvec {
    ( $( $x:expr ),* ) => {
        {
            let mut tmp = Vec::new();
            $( tmp.push($x); )*
            tmp
        }
    };
}
let v = myvec![4, 2, 9];  // the robot writes the pushes`,
lines:[
["macro_rules! myvec {","teach the robot a recipe"],
["    ( $( $x:expr ),* ) => {","recognize: any list of expressions, any length"],
["            let mut tmp = Vec::new();","the code the robot writes, part 1"],
["            $( tmp.push($x); )*","part 2: one push per item — * means \"repeat for each\""],
["            tmp","part 3: the whole block becomes the Vec"],
["let v = myvec![4, 2, 9];","one short line… the robot writes the rest"],
["// the robot writes the pushes","that's the whole trick"]
]},
tip:{h:"When do you need one?",b:"Almost never at first! Functions do 99 of 100 jobs. Take a macro when: the count is open (println!), or you must copy code, not take answers. Knowing the trick is enough."},
done:"Knows what a macro is"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"webserver", nav:"Building a web server",
title:"A web server — <em>with your own hands</em>",
lead:"The last build: a real listener for web lines. No framework. Plain TCP, a worker team, and real 'hello' flying over the net.",
words:[
"A web server is a host who always answers the door. <code class='inline'>TcpListener::bind(\"127.0.0.1:7878\")</code> sits at the address. <code class='inline'>.accept()</code> answers the next knock. Each knock is a new <code class='inline'>TcpStream</code> hello. Note: 7878 spells RUST on phone keys.",
"The knock follows a script: <code class='inline'>GET / HTTP/1.1</code> on the first line. We read it. We look at the path. Then we answer with an <b>HTTP answer</b>: a first line (<code class='inline'>HTTP/1.1 200 OK</code>), head lines like <code class='inline'>Content-Length</code>, and a body of HTML.",
"A 404 is just another first line plus a <i>Not Found</i> page. Browsers do not care much. You need two files next to <code class='inline'>main.rs</code> first: <code class='inline'>hello.html</code> and <code class='inline'>404.html</code>.",
"One guest at a time makes a long line. So we build a <b>worker team</b> (short name in code: <b>pool</b>). N workers share one line. The line is an <code class='inline'>mpsc</code> channel. Each worker loops: take a <i>Job</i>, run it. A Job is a <code class='inline'>Box&lt;dyn FnOnce&gt;</code> — any one-time job in the same box.",
"Last touch: <b>clean stop</b>. A Drop sends a 'go home' note to each worker. Then it waits for them. The server ends clean. And when a line drops, its door closes alone."
],
analogy:[["A host answering a ringing phone","=","TcpListener loop"],["Reading a guest's card first","=","reading GET / HTTP/1.1"],["A team sharing one job line","=","workers over an mpsc channel"]],
coach:"Box&lt;dyn FnOnce> looks big. It is just three ideas in one box. <b>dyn</b> = any job. <b>FnOnce</b> = runs one time. <b>Box</b> = the box that holds it. You met all three before. Only the mix is new.",
widget:{type:"gym", q:[
{q:"What does TcpListener::bind() do?", hint:"takes a door, waits there",
a:"It takes an address + port and waits there for knocks.", why:"bind gives a Result. Port taken? You see the error at once."},
{q:"Which HTTP request line does the Book's server answer with 200?", hint:"verb / path / version",
a:"GET / HTTP/1.1", why:"Every other request (POST, or a different path) gets the 404 response."},
{q:"Why does the worker team use Box&lt;dyn FnOnce> for a Job?", hint:"many jobs, one box kind",
a:"Each job is different, but the line must hold one kind — the box where any FnOnce fits.", why:"A Vec&lt;one kind> locks the line to one shape. dyn lets any one-time job in."},
{q:"What is the clean-stop trick?", hint:"the boss says goodbye",
a:"A Drop that sends a 'go home' note to each worker, then waits for them.", why:"It ends the line, turns no new jobs away rudely, and cleans the team instead of killing it."}
]},
code:{
copy:`let listener = TcpListener::bind("127.0.0.1:7878")?;
for stream in listener.incoming() {
    let stream = stream?;
    pool.execute(move || {
        let mut stream = stream;
        handle_connection(&mut stream);
    });
}`,
lines:[
["let listener = TcpListener::bind(\"127.0.0.1:7878\")?;","sit at the door, on port 7878"],
["for stream in listener.incoming() {","for every knock that rings…"],
["    let stream = stream?;","unwrap the handshake (or skip a noisy one)"],
["    pool.execute(move || {","give the whole guest to a free worker…"],
["        handle_connection(&mut stream);","…who reads the request and writes the response"],
["    });",""],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Two facts about 404:",b:"Browsers do not care much about 404s. The server still did its job — a right answer is worth more than a pretty page. And the real lesson: handle_connection wrote it all. First line, head lines, empty line, body. That is all of HTTP, in four lines."},
why:{kick:"why build a server by hand",
h:"After this lesson, no framework is magic. axum, actix, warp — all the same loop, nicer clothes.",
points:[
"<b>You met every std tool.</b> TcpStream = talk, Arc&lt;Mutex> = share, mpsc = notes, boxes for jobs. One project, the whole box.",
"<b>A team beats one thread per guest.</b> One thread per guest is fine for a toy. For real traffic it is too slow. This lesson shows why.",
"<b>Clean stop is pro finish.</b> Real services end lines, close doors, and log 'bye'. Now you know how."
]},
done:"Built a real TCP web server"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"cheats", nav:"Cheat sheet — operators & tools",
title:"The cheat sheet — <em>operators, tools, old vs new</em>",
lead:"The whole course on one page. Eight cards, one example, zero new ideas.",
words:[
"This is not new. It is all you typed, pressed into eight cards. Front: one sign or tool. Back: what it means, in plain words.",
"Try the cards first. Cover the answer. Guess out loud. Then read the example — all eight ideas at once.",
"Two tools keep your code clean, forever. <code class='inline'>cargo fmt</code> makes code pretty. <code class='inline'>cargo fix</code> fixes warnings alone. <code class='inline'>cargo clippy</code> is the teacher: run it with <code class='inline'>-D warnings</code>."
],
analogy:[["Packing only the clothes you wear","=","the cheat sheet"],["One page, every sign","=","eight cards, zero new ideas"]],
coach:"Pros keep this page open for years. Learn nothing by heart. Know where to look.",
widget:{type:"gym", q:[
{q:"`?` — pass bad up. What does it do?", hint:"bad goes up, good opens",
a:"Ok(v) opens into v. Err(e) goes back to the caller at once.", why:"One sign does five lines of match. New CLIs read clean with it."},
{q:"`..` or `..=` — which one keeps the end?", hint:"one more sign keeps it",
a:"`..` stops before the end: 1..4 is 1, 2, 3. `..=` keeps it: 1..=4 adds 4.", why:"Old Rust wrote 0...3. That no longer builds. NEW is 0..=3."},
{q:"println!(\"hi {x}\") — what is new?", hint:"the short way",
a:"Short format. No more \"hi {}\", x. Shorter, same answer.", why:"The OLD way still builds. The NEW way is what this course uses."},
{q:"Old `try!(x)` — what took its place?", hint:"one sign, same job",
a:"`x?` — try! is gone. ? does the same job.", why:"Old books show try!. Read it as ?. Same meaning."},
{q:"edition = \"2024\" in Cargo.toml — what is it?", hint:"new rules live here",
a:"The rule set your code follows. Old code still builds. New async and let-chains live in 2024.", why:"cargo fix --edition moves old code to new rules for you."},
{q:"`::` or `=>` or `|` — three signs, three jobs?", hint:"path, then, or",
a:":: is a path: std::fs. => is then-do in match arms. | is or: 1 | 2.", why:"Say them out loud: path, then, or. All three on every page."},
{q:"`&` or `*` or `_` — look, open, skip?", hint:"look, open, skip",
a:"& looks, no taking. * opens a box (or times). _ skips a value.", why:"&str borrows text. *b opens Box b. _ means 'no care'."},
{q:"cargo clippy or cargo fmt — who does what?", hint:"teacher or cleaner",
a:"clippy is the teacher: style notes and classic mistakes. fmt cleans the shape of code.", why:"Run both before you share code. Teams block bad clippy."}
]},
code:{
copy:`fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4];
    for n in &nums[1..=2] { // ..= keeps the end: 2, 3
        println!("{n}");    // NEW short format
    }
    let doubled: Vec<_> = nums.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();
    println!("{doubled:?}");
    Ok(()) // ? would pass errors up; here all is good
}`,
lines:[
["fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {","main can use ? — errors exit politely"],
["    let nums = vec![1, 2, 3, 4];","four numbers in a Vec"],
["    for n in &nums[1..=2] { // ..= keeps the end: 2, 3","borrow a slice; ..= keeps index 2"],
["        println!(\"{n}\");    // NEW short format","inline arg — no extra comma value"],
["    let doubled: Vec<_> = nums.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();","_ means: compiler, fill the type"],
["    println!(\"{doubled:?}\");","{:?} prints for debugging"],
["    Ok(()) // ? would pass errors up; here all is good","clean end — exit code 0"],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"Keywords — locked words:",b:"<code class='inline'>as async await break const continue crate dyn else enum extern false fn for if impl in let loop match mod move mut pub ref return Self self static struct super trait true type union unsafe use where while</code> — never use these as names. Must you? Write <code class='inline'>r#match</code>. New words like <code class='inline'>gen</code> and <code class='inline'>try</code> wait in line."},
why:{kick:"where the page lives in real work",
h:"Your tools want this style. clippy notes it. rustfmt makes it. Each crate you take expects it.",
points:[
"<b>clippy is the guard.</b> cargo clippy turns style into notes. It finds needless borrows and shadow slips.",
"<b>Ownership is the safety plan.</b> Safe memory with no trash truck is Rust's headline. This page IS that headline.",
"<b>The slice is all over.</b> &str, &[u8], &[T] — a window on data that is not yours. Half of std looks like this."
]},
done:"Keeps the cheat sheet by the keyboard"
}
);
;
RUST_LESSONS.push({
id:"glossary", nav:"Glossary — A to Z",
title:"The dictionary — <em>every word, one line each</em>",
lead:"Twenty-six words you met all course, now in one deck. Flip them like a passport: name on the front, meaning on the back.",
words:[
"Each card works the same. The hard word is on the front. The plain meaning is on the back.",
"Do not try to pass the deck. Skip cards you know. The ones you flip twice show your real gaps.",
"Card zero holds the whole course. Read the deck once a month."
],
analogy:[["A passport, one stamp per land","=","one card per word"],["One card per word, A to Z","=","the full deck in order"]],
coach:"A glossary is for knowing, not memorizing. You should say 'I met this word — one owner per value'. Not read the book from memory.",
widget:{type:"gym", q:[
{q:"A — Arc", hint:"one toy, shared with every thread",
a:"Rc that threads may share. Arc::clone(&x) shares one value.", why:"Rc lives in one thread only. Arc counts safe, so many threads can hold on."},
{q:"B — Borrow &", hint:"look, don't own",
a:"Look, don't own. & to read. &mut to change.", why:"Many readers or one writer — never both. The compiler counts."},
{q:"C — Cargo", hint:"the tool that builds",
a:"The builder. new, run, check, build, test, doc, publish.", why:"One tool for a project's whole life. Use cargo, not rustc alone."},
{q:"D — Deref *", hint:"open the box",
a:"Open the box. Auto change: &Box&lt;String> becomes &str for you.", why:"Functions that want &str accept your Box too. No &(*m)[..] needed."},
{q:"E — Enum", hint:"one shape at a time",
a:"One shape at a time. Option ends null forever.", why:"Some(x) or None — no null hiding anywhere. match must cover every shape."},
{q:"F — Future, async", hint:"a sleepy job",
a:"A sleepy job. It needs .await GO plus a runner to wake it.", why:"Call an async fn: food ordered. .await: sit down and eat."},
{q:"G — Generics &lt;T>", hint:"the empty box",
a:"An empty box for any kind. No extra cost when it runs.", why:"Vec&lt;T> and Option&lt;T> are written once. They take every T, full speed."},
{q:"H — HashMap", hint:"labels on jars",
a:"Key to value, no order. entry().or_insert() counts in one line.", why:"Need 'how many times did I see X'? The entry API is the whole trick."},
{q:"I — Impl", hint:"skills for your struct",
a:"Skills for a struct or enum. &self reads. &mut self changes.", why:"Data sits in struct. Skills live in impl. That split is Rust's way."},
{q:"J — Join", hint:"wait for the worker",
a:"handle.join() waits for a thread to end.", why:"No join: main goes home early and the new thread never ends."},
{q:"K — Keyword mut", hint:"the change word",
a:"You can change it. let is locked. let mut is open.", why:"mut is a bright flag: CHANGES HERE. Readers search it first."},
{q:"L — Lifetime 'a", hint:"how long the look lives",
a:"How long the borrowed look stays valid.", why:"It stops a pointer to a dead thing. Mostly the compiler fills it in."},
{q:"M — Match", hint:"check every shape",
a:"Check all cases. if let is the short form for one case.", why:"Miss a branch? That is a build error, not a night bug."},
{q:"N — None / Some", hint:"empty or full",
a:"The maybe-box: empty (None) or full (Some).", why:"Two shapes, always handled. This pair killed the null pointer."},
{q:"O — Ownership", hint:"one box, one owner",
a:"One owner per value. Trash comes at }.", why:"Move means the old spot is done. Small kinds like i32 just copy."},
{q:"P — Panic", hint:"stop now",
a:"Stop now, crash loudly. Result is the one you can fix.", why:"Panic is for bugs that must never happen. Result is for errors users cause."},
{q:"Q — ?", hint:"bad goes up, good opens",
a:"Ok(v) opens into v. Err(e) goes back to the caller at once.", why:"Five lines of match turn into one sign. New tools read clean with it."},
{q:"R — Rc", hint:"count the owners",
a:"Counts owners. Ends at 0. One thread only.", why:"Rc::clone(&a) adds a count, not a copy. Threads need Arc."},
{q:"S — Slice &s[0..2]", hint:"see a part, own nothing",
a:"See a part, own nothing. &str is the slice for text.", why:"Slices borrow a window. The owner keeps the whole house."},
{q:"T — Trait", hint:"a shared skill",
a:"A shared skill. impl Summary for Post keeps the promise.", why:"Same skill, many kinds. Generics run fast with one kind. dyn mixes many."},
{q:"U — Unsafe", hint:"the door you open yourself",
a:"Five extra powers. Keep the block small, wrap it safe outside.", why:"Raw speed, C code, tiny chips. No unsafe in year one unless the book says."},
{q:"V — Vec", hint:"a shelf that grows",
a:"A list that grows: vec![], push, and get for safe reads.", why:"The normal shelf. Out-of-range reads stop — no silent bad data."},
{q:"W — Where", hint:"long rules go last",
a:"where T: Display moves long rules out of the brackets.", why:"Same promise as &lt;T: Display>. Easier to read when rules grow."},
{q:"X — dyn", hint:"the zoo cage",
a:"Box&lt;dyn Draw> holds many kinds in one box.", why:"Enums need all kinds up front. dyn lets new kinds in with no old change."},
{q:"Y — Yank", hint:"hide the bad box you shared",
a:"cargo yank hides a bad version you shared.", why:"Old builds keep working. No one new can take the bad one."},
{q:"Z — ()", hint:"empty hands",
a:"Empty hands: no value. The unit type.", why:"Functions that return nothing return (). Even println! gives you ()."}
]},
code:{
copy:`let pile: Vec<&str> = vec!["arc", "borrow", "cargo", "dyn"];
for term in pile {
    println!("- {term}");
}`,
lines:[
["let pile: Vec<&str> = vec![\"arc\", \"borrow\", \"cargo\", \"dyn\"];","a Vec can hold dictionary words"],
["for term in pile {","visit every entry"],
["    println!(\"- {term}\");","print it"],
["}",""]
]},
tip:{h:"A to Z — climb or search:",b:"The deck runs Arc to (). Same order as the book's last page. Flip any card. The back always shows the why."},
why:{kick:"why keep two word lists",
h:"You have this small deck AND the full Rust manual. The deck is how you talk. The manual is how you check.",
points:[
"<b>Know, don't memorize.</b> A glossary keeps words near while you work. rustc and docs fill the gaps while you code.",
"<b>Words are tools.</b> Real Rust code uses crates and kinds named with these words. Know the word, find the tool.",
"<b>Ownership is first.</b> A hard Rust sentence? It is almost always ownership — or lifetimes. Flip those cards again."
]},
done:"Can explain the core words, A to Z"
}
);
;
