GENERAL_LESSONS.push({
id:"heaps", nav:"Heaps & priority queues",
title:"Heaps & priority queues — <em>the hospital line</em>",
lead:"A line where the most urgent patient is always served first — and it stays fast no matter how long the line gets.",
words:[
"A <b>heap</b> is a line with one rule: <b>the smallest item always sits at the front</b> (that's a <b>min-heap</b>; flip the rule and you get a max-heap). The hospital triage line works exactly this way — the most urgent patient goes first. Computer scientists call it a <b>priority queue</b>.",
"Here's the clever part: a heap is a <b>tree hiding inside a list</b>. The rule is that a parent is always smaller than its children. Because of that rule, the front (index 0) is always the smallest thing in the list — you never have to hunt for it. The hospital picture ends here: a real line doesn't bubble — <b>the tree part does</b>. The line only answers <i>who's first</i>; the bubbles answer <i>how we keep the smallest first</i>.",
"When you <b>push</b>: drop the new item at the end, then <b>bubble up</b> — swap with your parent until you're not smaller than them anymore. When you <b>pop the min</b>: take the front, move the last item up to the front, then <b>bubble down</b> — swap with the smaller child until the rule holds again. Two operations, two bubbles.",
"Both bubbles take <b>O(log n)</b> hops — just the height of the tree. A thousand items, ten hops. A million, twenty. That's the superpower: task schedulers, Dijkstra's shortest path, and every \"top 10\" problem run on heaps. In Rust: <code class='inline'>BinaryHeap</code> (a max-heap) — wrap values in <code class='inline'>Reverse</code> for a min-heap.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>smallest always on top. Push = bubble up. Pop = bubble down. A tree hiding in a list — log n hops.</i>",
"🔮 <b>Predict before you push:</b> the first patient is 4, and the line starts [3, 8, 5]. Where will 4 land? Guess, then press Push and watch the bubble — right or wrong, the rule does the rest."
],
analogy:[["The hospital line — most urgent first","=","Priority queue"],["The smallest is always at the front","=","The heap rule"],["A new patient bubbles up to their spot","=","push — O(log n)"],["Treat the front, the last one bubbles down","=","pop-min — O(log n)"]],
widget:{type:"heaps"},
code:{
copy:`use std::collections::BinaryHeap;
use std::cmp::Reverse;

let mut pq = BinaryHeap::new();   // max-heap: biggest first
pq.push(3);
pq.push(1);
pq.push(2);
let top = pq.pop();               // Some(3) — biggest leaves first

let mut minq = BinaryHeap::new(); // min-heap: wrap in Reverse
minq.push(Reverse(1));
minq.pop();                       // Reverse(1) — smallest first`,
lines:[
["use std::collections::BinaryHeap;","the heap toolbox — biggest first"],
["use std::cmp::Reverse;","the tiny wrapper that flips the rule"],
["let mut pq = BinaryHeap::new();","an empty hospital line"],
["pq.push(1);","a patient arrives, bubbles to their spot"],
["let top = pq.pop();","Some(3) — the MOST urgent leaves first"],
["minq.push(Reverse(1));","wrap values to get a min-heap"],
["minq.pop();","Reverse(1) — the SMALLEST leaves first"]
]},
tip:{h:"One sentence:",b:"<b>Biggest first → BinaryHeap.</b> Smallest first → <b>wrap it in Reverse.</b> Push and pop both take log n hops — the tree never gets taller than that."},
done:"Heaps up a priority queue"
}
);
;
GENERAL_LESSONS.push({
id:"mergesort", nav:"Merge sort",
title:"Merge sort — <em>divide & conquer</em>",
lead:"The sorting algorithm that promises: always fast, no bad days. It works with one idea used twice.",
words:[
"Merge sort runs on one move: <b>split the list in half, sort each half, then merge</b> the two sorted halves back into one sorted list. Sounds circular — it is, and that's the point. The base case saves you: <b>a list of one is already sorted</b>.",
"So: keep splitting until every pile has exactly one card. Then merge your way back up. Merging two sorted piles is one clever loop: <b>always take the smaller front card</b>. Watch: <b>[1, 4]</b> and <b>[2, 3]</b> → 1 vs 2, take 1; 4 vs 2, take 2; take 3, then 4 → <b>[1, 2, 3, 4]</b>. One pass, sorted.",
"Because each pile is sorted, the merge never has to look back — the smallest remaining card is always one of the two fronts. That's why merging is so cheap: two front fingers, sliding together like a zipper. The deck picture ends here: real lists don't physically split — <b>the split is just how you think about it</b>. What's real is the merge: two sorted piles, one zipper pass.",
"How fast? Every card is touched once per level, and the list halves each level: <b>O(n log n)</b>, every time, no exceptions. Even a worst-case shuffled deck is as fast as a nearly sorted one. Rust's <code class='inline'>sort()</code> is a merge-sort cousin — stable and calm.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>split to one card, then merge sorted piles — always take the smaller front. n log n, no matter what.</i>",
"🔮 <b>Predict before you play:</b> the deck starts [8, 3, 7, 1, 9, 2, 5, 4]. Guess the first merged pile — the zipper always takes the smaller front. Then press Play."
],
analogy:[["Split the deck until one card each","=","Divide"],["Two sorted piles become one line","=","Merge — smaller front first"],["Split, solve, combine","=","Divide & conquer"],["Every card touched once per level","=","O(n log n)"]],
widget:{type:"mergesort"},
code:{
copy:`fn merge(a: &[i32], b: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {
    let mut out = Vec::new();
    let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);
    while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {
        if a[i] <= b[j] { out.push(a[i]); i += 1; }
        else            { out.push(b[j]); j += 1; }
    }
    out.extend(&a[i..]);
    out.extend(&b[j..]);
    out
}`,
lines:[
["fn merge(a: &[i32], b: &[i32]) -> Vec<i32> {","two sorted piles in, one sorted pile out"],
["let (mut i, mut j) = (0, 0);","two front fingers — the smallest of each pile"],
["while i < a.len() && j < b.len() {","keep zipping while both piles have cards"],
["if a[i] <= b[j] { out.push(a[i]); i += 1; }","take the smaller FRONT card, advance"],
["else            { out.push(b[j]); j += 1; }","or the other front — always the smaller"],
["out.extend(&a[i..]);","leftovers are already sorted — glue them on"],
["out.extend(&b[j..]);","same for the other pile"]
]},
tip:{h:"The whole trick:",b:"<b>One card is already sorted.</b> Split until you get there, then merge sorted piles by always taking the smaller front. Sorted in, sorted out — <b>O(n log n)</b>, always."},
done:"Divides & conquers"
}
);
;
GENERAL_LESSONS.push({
id:"backtrack", nav:"Backtracking",
title:"Backtracking — <em>the maze walker</em>",
lead:"Try a path. If it dead-ends, walk back to the last fork and try the next one. Exhaustively, calmly.",
words:[
"Backtracking is how you solve a maze with your finger: <b>walk forward</b> while you can, and when you hit a dead end, <b>walk back to the last fork and try the other way</b>. No magic, no map — just patience and a memory of where you've been.",
"It's depth-first search with a rule: <b>mark every cell you visit</b>. When a cell has no unvisited exits, it's a dead end — and the path behind you (the stack) remembers the way back to the fork. The finger picture has a blind spot: you can see the whole maze, the walker can't — it only knows the cell it's standing in. It never <i>sees</i> a dead end coming; it must walk in, mark it, and walk back out.",
"The word 'backtracking' means the step most searches skip: when you retreat, <b>you un-choose</b>. In puzzles you try to place something — a queen, a number, a tile. If it breaks the rules, remove it and try the next candidate. The 'remove' line is the heart of the algorithm.",
"That's the engine behind the N-Queens puzzle, Sudoku solvers, and even Rust's <code class='inline'>?</code> operator — a function that fails just walks back up the call stack to the last fork and lets the caller try another way.",
"🗣️ <b>Say it out loud:</b> <i>try, mark, dead end → walk back, un-choose, try the next. The path behind you is the stack.</i>",
"🔮 <b>Predict before you walk:</b> the explorer always tries right first. Guess which cell gets painted red first — then press Walk and check. The two red cells are the two planted traps."
],
analogy:[["Finger on the maze wall","=","Walk forward"],["Dead end → back to the last fork","=","Backtrack"],["Remove what doesn't fit, try the next","=","Sudoku, N-Queens"],["The path behind you","=","A call stack"]],
widget:{type:"backtrack"},
code:{
copy:`fn solve(maze: &mut Vec<Vec<char>>, r: usize, c: usize) -> bool {
    if r == maze.len() - 1 && c == maze[0].len() - 1 { return true; }
    for (dr, dc) in [(0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, 0)] {
        let (nr, nc) = (r as i32 + dr, c as i32 + dc);
        if nr < 0 || nc < 0 { continue; }
        let (nr, nc) = (nr as usize, nc as usize);
        if maze[nr][nc] != '.' { continue; }     // wall or already visited
        maze[nr][nc] = '*';                       // mark: I'm walking here
        if solve(maze, nr, nc) { return true; }   // trust the smaller maze
        maze[nr][nc] = '.';                       // dead end — UN-choose
    }
    false
}`,
lines:[
["fn solve(maze: &mut Vec<Vec<char>>, r: usize, c: usize) -> bool {","try cell (r, c) — am I at the exit yet?"],
["if r == maze.len() - 1 && c == maze[0].len() - 1 { return true; }","bottom-right corner — found it!"],
["for (dr, dc) in [(0, 1), (1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, 0)] {","try right, down, left, up"],
["if maze[nr][nc] != '.' { continue; }","wall or already visited — skip"],
["maze[nr][nc] = '*';","mark: I'm walking here"],
["if solve(maze, nr, nc) { return true; }","trust the smaller maze"],
["maze[nr][nc] = '.';","dead end — UN-choose, walk back"]
]},
tip:{h:"The backbone:",b:"<b>Try → mark → recurse → if it fails, un-mark and try the next.</b> The 'un-mark' line is what makes it backtracking. Remove it and the maze fills with wrong paths."},
done:"Backtracks with grace"
}
);
